    <node id="&quot;STUDENT&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"PERSON"</data>
      <data key="d1">The STUDENT is an individual learner who submits program code and associated error descriptions as part of an academic or institutional assessment process, typically in response to a specific educational problem—such as grid-based graph traversal using BFS, array manipulation, or entity extraction tasks. The STUDENT’s understanding of core computational concepts—including control flow, array traversal, conditional checks, boolean flag management, and search logic—is evaluated solely based on the presence, type, and context of errors in their submission. These errors serve as diagnostic indicators of conceptual misunderstandings or gaps in knowledge, allowing evaluators to infer the STUDENT’s mastery level, which is categorized as either “Good” or “Bad” depending on the alignment of their code with the expected correct solution.

The assessment is conducted independently for each submission, often using system-recorded data such as Record ID 72-1 (logged on January 23, 2025), and is designed not merely to judge correctness but to identify specific deficiencies in the STUDENT’s conceptual understanding. While the STUDENT remains an unnamed or unspecified individual across all descriptions, their role is central to the evaluation framework, with each submission treated as a discrete measure of learning and competence. The evaluation process is intentionally focused on error analysis—such as mismanaged conditional flags or flawed array traversal patterns—to inform targeted academic interventions and guide instructional improvements. Ultimately, the STUDENT’s performance is not judged by identity or effort, but by the observable outcomes of their code, making their submission the primary artifact through which their understanding is measured and inferred.</data>
