    <edge source="&quot;STUDENT PROGRAM&quot;" target="&quot;CONTROL_STRUCTURE&quot;">
      <data key="d3">142.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The Student Program uses if-statements and loops, but contains a fatal error: exit(1) for m==1, which terminates the program instead of outputting ‘NO’, showing a fundamental misunderstanding of control flow."&lt;SEP&gt;"The program uses control structures (if, for loops) but contains critical errors such as initializing m = n and incorrect bounds checking, showing misunderstanding of conditional logic."&lt;SEP&gt;The Student Program employs control structures—including if-statements, nested for-loops, and while loops—to manage program flow, control the movement and positioning of elements (particularly the placement of '1's in a matrix), and handle termination conditions such as multiple test cases. These structures are used to direct conditional updates of state variables cnti and cntj, which are intended to track target positions during traversal. While the student demonstrates a basic understanding of control flow by implementing these structures—evidenced by correct use of a while loop for multiple test cases—the overall logic is fundamentally flawed. 

The program contains multiple logical errors in the formulation of conditional constraints (e.g., rejecting valid inputs when m = 1 or when n - m &gt; 26), incorrect reset logic (e.g., setting cntj = 1 after n/2), and improper incrementation of cnti and cntj, which lead to misalignment of target positions, incorrect matrix manipulation, and even infinite loops. Additionally, flawed loop bounds and state management cause the algorithm to traverse incorrectly, resulting in misplaced '1's and false "NO" outputs for valid inputs. Although the student correctly applies the syntactic form of control structures, the underlying control flow logic is inconsistent with the problem’s requirements, revealing a poor understanding of the logical constraints and state transitions necessary for correct program behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;"The student's control structures (if, for, exit) are used to handle edge cases and loops, but are misapplied, leading to incorrect program behavior."&lt;SEP&gt;"The student correctly structured the test case loop (while(T--)), indicating proper grasp of control flow, though the solve() function logic is flawed."&lt;SEP&gt;"The student uses control structures (if, for loops) but misuses exit(1) for a valid edge case (m==1), showing incorrect handling of program branching and termination conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"The student's code relies on nested if-statements and loops to control logic flow, suggesting an attempt to apply control structures — but the error implies misunderstanding in their application."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-fa0781f7cefac10e68ae2462b8b87c96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bdb8ee034f3f855cc75e56f09fb5d38e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-465db4ec94fa4f2a3c4c7e2cee8d5ddb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ede3f2ca6120cc400df50c87dd9d424e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-863e6c43b922d66931de72f56a647cc3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ab516531ae70002b07e7282cf9f96ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-57aa2fab03045a4f1a94b22bda9b2d8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c9e21c9bd682aec8ed5433b29254d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-886d290d1e72e5c4612a7a2f92b3deda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a6025949968addfd629ffba156975574&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c900fee450edfcc61df8a5dc49da1d44&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8aa247fbd5005d5da8b7a8cde9c6045&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ba71498d3f8470570296343c8c189b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc7bcf65aa4562f02e59c3d8969ea961&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a57838963dbbc9472322e841bbb48518&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4bfa74d3cde642ef5d69d9445bbe6168&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1bb26b30f621e23f24679fbe24e9ff02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-751de14de4f6fd0795902684f69fa4d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34f50176f8b78d245242c999fcfe00f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bf87c7e9ad9d433d6f2ea0161bd510c8</data>
