    <edge source="&quot;HASH&quot;" target="&quot;硝基甲苯之袭&quot;">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The use of Hash (frequency counting) in the student's code is a critical optimization technique applied to solve the 硝基甲苯之袭 problem efficiently."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-0912d8b8a7e5a597e50db3ea5336268a</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HASH&quot;" target="&quot;小苯&quot;">
      <data key="d3">25.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"小苯 correctly applies hashing via the cnt[] array to count frequencies, enabling efficient lookup of XOR complements — this part of the solution is sound."&lt;SEP&gt;"小苯 correctly uses a hash table (cnt array) to count element frequencies, enabling efficient lookup of potential complement values (a[i] XOR j), demonstrating solid understanding of hash-based optimization."&lt;SEP&gt;"小苯 uses a frequency array 'cnt[]' to hash and count occurrences of each integer, enabling efficient multiplication of pair counts — a correct application of hashing for optimization."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-c0621f867e87fb25088a534465259d65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff32fffb52415ec4221990a56352b068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d78f2079f220a0196b9a21caa622db85</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HASH&quot;" target="&quot;NUMBER_THEORY&quot;">
      <data key="d3">70.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Hash functions commonly rely on Number_Theory (e.g., prime modulus, modular hashing) to ensure uniform distribution and minimize collisions."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hash functions often rely on Number_Theory principles (e.g., modular arithmetic, prime numbers) for distribution and collision resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hash is employed to optimize the enumeration of pairs under Number_Theory constraints, reducing redundant calculations by counting frequency of values that satisfy GCD-XOR conditions."&lt;SEP&gt;"While hashing efficiently counts occurrences, it cannot compensate for the underlying number-theoretic misunderstanding in the condition a_i XOR a_j == gcd(a_i, a_j), leading to correct data structures but incorrect logic."&lt;SEP&gt;"Number_Theory provides the mathematical foundation for many cryptographic hash functions, such as those relying on prime numbers or modular inverses, making their conceptual relationship strong."&lt;SEP&gt;"Number_Theory and Hash are often interrelated in cryptography and data integrity, where modular arithmetic underpins hash function design."&lt;SEP&gt;"The design and security of hash functions depend on Number_Theory concepts like primality and modular arithmetic, making this a foundational dependency."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hash functions often rely on number-theoretic principles such as modular arithmetic and prime number properties for distribution and collision resistance."&lt;SEP&gt;"Hash functions in competitive programming often use modular arithmetic from number theory to avoid collisions and ensure uniform distribution."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-bb9ecea8e462df8b8d62bff75dfff387&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd3a2cb8082fc545af89c60f79efbd13&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2c44d59ce1bad68da6631b1fbb2adb8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e14c1d7f404ce9578e01e19f11a846b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff32fffb52415ec4221990a56352b068&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0621f867e87fb25088a534465259d65&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b61b86f3541688082eb6cab0bf35563</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;HASH&quot;" target="&quot;双生数组&quot;">
      <data key="d3">7.0</data>
