      <data key="d5">chunk-0912d8b8a7e5a597e50db3ea5336268a</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;GCD&quot;">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Each a_i is involved in computing gcd(a_i, a_j) for all valid j, making it a direct operand of the gcd operation."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-0912d8b8a7e5a597e50db3ea5336268a</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;XOR&quot;">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"Each a_i participates in the xor operation with a_j to produce the left-hand side of the constraint equation."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-0912d8b8a7e5a597e50db3ea5336268a</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;A_J&quot;">
      <data key="d3">9.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"a_i and a_j are pairs of elements selected from the array with i &lt; j, and their relationship is defined by the condition a_i xor a_j = gcd(a_i, a_j), which constrains valid combinations in the numerical space."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-292b69dd280ff7c3c431c909f2fa1f47</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;CNT[A[I]]&quot;">
      <data key="d3">8.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The frequency array cnt maps each unique value a_i to its count, enabling the algorithm to compute pair contributions multiplicatively rather than iteratively, optimizing the search over the numerical domain."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-292b69dd280ff7c3c431c909f2fa1f47</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;M&quot;">
      <data key="d3">6.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"M defines the maximum possible value range for array elements via memory allocation, implicitly bounding the values a_i can take and ensuring the cnt array can index all possible integers in the problem space."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-292b69dd280ff7c3c431c909f2fa1f47</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;L_I&quot;">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"a_i is a random variable whose possible values are constrained by the interval [l_i, r_i], making l_i a critical boundary parameter for a_i’s distribution."&lt;SEP&gt;"l_i sets the minimum possible value for a_i, directly influencing the range and expected behavior of the absolute differences in val(a)."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-b596842b3f70ab70e198d8ac72878311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a84c0c02b3b7d3ef7caf0684c1b178</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;A_I&quot;" target="&quot;R_I&quot;">
      <data key="d3">16.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"a_i is a random variable whose possible values are constrained by the interval [l_i, r_i], making r_i a critical boundary parameter for a_i’s distribution."&lt;SEP&gt;"r_i sets the maximum possible value for a_i, directly influencing the range and expected behavior of the absolute differences in val(a)."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-b596842b3f70ab70e198d8ac72878311&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20a84c0c02b3b7d3ef7caf0684c1b178</data>
      <data key="d6">1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;GCD&quot;" target="&quot;XOR&quot;">
      <data key="d3">20.0</data>
      <data key="d4">"The problem requires that GCD and XOR of two numbers be equal — a rare and non-trivial number-theoretic relationship that only holds under specific conditions, such as when one number is a multiple of the other and their difference equals their GCD."&lt;SEP&gt;"gcd and xor are the two core mathematical operations whose equality defines the problem's constraint, making them interdependent in the solution logic."</data>
      <data key="d5">chunk-0912d8b8a7e5a597e50db3ea5336268a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d78f2079f220a0196b9a21caa622db85</data>
